# Does chalk fizz in vinegar? A two-vessel acid control
#
# Use only ordinary 5% food vinegar and calcium-carbonate classroom chalk in a
# real activity. Wear goggles, protect the table, ask an adult first, and never
# taste anything that has been placed in lab glassware.
register lv1

# Control: a chalk stick in the same volume of plain water barely dissolves and
# has no finite acid source with which to make a visible burst of carbon dioxide.
add v1 water 50mL
add v1 chalk_stick 2g
inspect v1

# Test: change water to the named 5% vinegar recipe and keep the chalk dose the
# same. The balanced reaction consumes finite CaCO3 and acetic acid. Its real
# CO2 inventory drives the normal bubble effect and leaves through this open
# beaker; it is not a canned animation.
new beaker
add v2 white_vinegar_5_percent 50mL
add v2 chalk_stick 2g
inspect v2

# Try a smaller chalk dose or less vinegar in a fresh vessel. The limiting
# reagent changes the gas amount. Powdering would change the real-world rate,
# but particle-area kinetics for this reaction remain an explicit future model.
